content.text:
This example works with the current code:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"log"

	sandbox "github.com/sandbox-kit/kit"
)

// Your sandbox adapter implements the generated interface.
type MySandboxAdapter struct{}

func (*MySandboxAdapter) Info() *sandbox.SandboxAdapterInfo {
	return &sandbox.SandboxAdapterInfo{
		Provider: "my-sandbox",
	}
}

// Your harness adapter implements its own generated interface.
type MyHarnessAdapter struct{}

func (*MyHarnessAdapter) Info() *sandbox.HarnessAdapterInfo {
	return &sandbox.HarnessAdapterInfo{
		Provider: "my-harness",
	}
}

func main() {
	client, err := sandbox.NewClient(
		&MySandboxAdapter{},
		sandbox.WithHarnessAdapter(&MyHarnessAdapter{}),
	)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	fmt.Println(client.Sandbox().Info().GetProvider())
	fmt.Println(client.Harness().Info().GetProvider())
}
```

Output:

```text
my-sandbox
my-harness
```

You can also create a client without a harness:

```go
client, err := sandbox.NewClient(&MySandboxAdapter{})
```

This demonstrates adapter injection and validation. It registers local adapters; actual sandbox creation, execution, and snapshots will come in later increments.

The repository already has a runnable equivalent in [example_test.go](/Users/nabhag/OpenSource/kit/example_test.go). Run it with:

```sh
go test -run ExampleNewClient -v .
```

Yes—generators can produce **structs/classes, constructors, and implemented methods**, not just interfaces.

The distinction is what those methods do:

| Generator | Generated code |
|---|---|
| Protobuf | Data structs/classes, getters, serialization and reflection methods |
| gRPC / Connect | Client structs and methods that make network calls |
| Custom generator | Client structs, constructors, and methods that delegate to injected adapters |

Protobuf already generates structs and supporting methods. [Go generated-code guide](https://protobuf.dev/reference/go/go-generated/)

For your design, a custom generator can generate something like:

```go
type Client struct {
    adapter SandboxAdapter
}

func NewClient(adapter SandboxAdapter) *Client {
    return &Client{adapter: adapter}
}

func (c *Client) Create(ctx context.Context, request *CreateRequest) (...) {
    return c.adapter.Create(ctx, request)
}
```

The method implementation above is mechanical delegation, so it can be generated. Mapping that operation to Modal or Daytona requires provider-specific instructions or implementation.

**Our current custom generator only emits interfaces.** It should also generate the client struct, constructor, and delegation methods as we introduce operation contracts. That is the generation approach you’ve been asking for.
